#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171837 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172031 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbac348b-629f-52a1-9bb3-0d3f8483ff44" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Arrow Function Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "2b0e57b0-a667-5bb2-bb1e-69c4d39b799e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8474" :question/problem "Which of the following functions has the codomain $[0,1]?$\n\nI. $p: \\{ 0, 1 \\} \\to \\mathbb{R}$\n\nII. $q: \\mathbb{R} \\to [ 0, 1 ]$\n\nIII. $r: [0,1] \\to [ 0, 1 )$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $f: X \\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- The codomain of $p$ is $\\mathbb{R},$ the set of real numbers.\n\n- The codomain of $q$ is $[ 0, 1 ],$ the interval from $0$ and $1$ (including $0$ and $1$).\n\n- The codomain of $r$ is $[ 0, 1),$ the interval from $0$ to $1$ (including $0$ but not including $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171838 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172032 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fdf7e44-a092-5990-a10e-d48b175dcef0" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Function Using Arrow Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "686f5d13-dfe9-5434-abff-052c7a4b76ba" :question/math-academy-id "e-8475" :question/problem "Which of the following is the correct symbolic notation for the sentence given below?\n\n$\\qquad$ *\"The function $g$ maps the set of rational numbers to the set $Y$.\"*\n\nI. $g:\\mathbb{Q} \\to Y$\n\nII. $g: Y \\to \\mathbb{Q}$\n\nIII. $g:\\mathbb{R} \\to Y$" :question/worked-solution "The sentence *\"The function $g$ maps the set of rational numbers to the set $Y$\"* is equivalent to the following symbolic notation:\n\n$\\qquad$ $g:\\mathbb{Q} \\to Y$\n\nHere, the domain of $g$ is $\\mathbb Q,$ and the codomain of $g$ is $Y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171839 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172033 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e61a3c8-45b0-532e-a54f-4597ad01a6f0" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Alternative Arrow Notations"}] :question/id #uuid "c02bc2d0-fccb-5fbe-8914-c42ebc7bb9fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-8476" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $g(x)=x-x^3?$\n\nI. $g:x \\mapsto x-x^3$\n\nII. $g:x \\mapsto -x^3$\n\nIII. $g:-x \\mapsto x^3$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $g(x)=x-x^3$ means that the function $g$ maps the element $x$ in the domain to $x-x^3$ in the codomain. This can also be written as\n\n$$\ng:x \\mapsto x-x^3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171840 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172034 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1240e42a-92a5-51cc-a80e-3fe1ef0b16f7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Codomains and Ranges"}] :question/id #uuid "086ea590-785a-539d-8b0f-b69ac7301ce4" :question/math-academy-id "e-8487" :question/problem "Which of the following is the correct interpretation of $g: \\{ -1,0,1 \\} \\to \\mathbb{R}?$\n\nI. The domain of $g$ is $\\{ -1,0,1 \\}$\n\nII. The codomain of $g$ is $\\mathbb{R}$\n\nIII. The range of $g$ is $\\mathbb{R}$" :question/worked-solution "The notation \n\n$$\ng: \\{ -1,0,1 \\} \\to \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\nmeans that the function $g$ maps the set $\\{ -1,0,1 \\}$ to the set $\\mathbb{R}.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $g$ is $\\{ -1,0,1 \\},$ and\n\n- the codomain of $g$ is $\\mathbb{R}.$ Note that this is *not* necessarily the range.\n\nAs a result, statements I and II are true, while statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559888 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172032 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fdf7e44-a092-5990-a10e-d48b175dcef0" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Function Using Arrow Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b61bd54-1c7d-591e-939b-77de4327abc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h:\\{0,1\\}\\to \\{0,1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559040} {:answer/id #uuid "1035bbb6-7038-5b32-98d1-d43d0cee1aa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h:(0,1)\\to (0,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559054} {:answer/id #uuid "43bcd4c6-a1f1-5472-bf3f-22916bfd9d61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h:(0,1)\\to [0,1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559193} {:answer/id #uuid "541ffd3e-8a84-5a4f-bd1a-89894363b8da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h:(0,1)\\to \\{0,1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559230} {:answer/id #uuid "e126d013-b0a4-5d2c-bf12-3c1d12a00d91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h:\\{0,1\\}\\to (0,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559572}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "541ffd3e-8a84-5a4f-bd1a-89894363b8da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h:(0,1)\\to \\{0,1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559230} :answer-field/id #uuid "7732c4e3-3161-5802-ab59-a7cdbdfc5997" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f9c7a6ed-7bc4-5fae-bd01-cf7380224c4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-104937" :question/problem "Which of the following is the correct symbolic notation for the sentence given below?\n\n*\"The function $h$ maps the interval from $0$ to $1$ exclusive to the set containing the two numbers $0$ and $1$ only.\"*" :question/worked-solution "The sentence *\"The function $h$ maps the interval from $0$ to $1$ exclusive to the set containing the two numbers $0$ and $1$ only\"* is equivalent to the following symbolic notation:\n\n$\\,$ $h:(0,1)\\to \\{0,1\\}$\n\nHere, the domain of $h$ is $(0,1),$ and the codomain of $h$ is $\\{0,1\\}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559832 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172033 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e61a3c8-45b0-532e-a54f-4597ad01a6f0" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Alternative Arrow Notations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aacc03f-d443-56a8-98ec-3789fe421eda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h:x↦2{x}^{3}-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559037} {:answer/id #uuid "554769e0-913b-5666-8b52-da7a146ba940" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h:x↦-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559233} {:answer/id #uuid "783a5b80-d714-5773-9afd-24db9211e58b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h:3x↦2{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559317} {:answer/id #uuid "88ecda15-e589-5fe6-ad21-b805845401c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h:x↦{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559360} {:answer/id #uuid "a040e295-4426-5498-a1ec-eee2127b6a9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h:2{x}^{3}↦-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559422}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0aacc03f-d443-56a8-98ec-3789fe421eda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "h:x↦2{x}^{3}-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559037} :answer-field/id #uuid "86cbd155-e168-55e6-b312-a580ef748d0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "97bd3e47-4a7d-51fe-87e4-ccb53cfbd7e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-104941" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $h(x)=2{x}^{3}-3x?$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $h(x)=2{x}^{3}-3x$ means that the function $h$ maps the element $x$ in the domain to $2{x}^{3}-3x$ in the codomain. This can also be written as\n\n$$\nh:x↦2{x}^{3}-3x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559844 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172034 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1240e42a-92a5-51cc-a80e-3fe1ef0b16f7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Codomains and Ranges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a37a47e-df4b-5a3b-b91d-251a23cdbc67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559036} {:answer/id #uuid "1aa6843f-235e-55f5-a1d2-65b5255eca12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559076} {:answer/id #uuid "2bf5c615-bac0-5e6d-bed3-fad212104c62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559126} {:answer/id #uuid "84ab3d78-f997-5627-8d22-8f29bbd35fd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559349} {:answer/id #uuid "9ce61f7b-f3ab-56c7-96d3-2117a364b564" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ce61f7b-f3ab-56c7-96d3-2117a364b564" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559415} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b5c1a5d-7ac0-5268-a351-1c74d654d1fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559655}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "af103ef7-b89b-5289-9d5d-02c1e186f66e" :question/math-academy-id "q-104951" :question/problem "![](images/6a/6ac4933860ceea29b5892d1ea38685dd7d7fbb5e1a6a48cda45b48151b35aeb1.png)\n\nConsider the function $g:X\\to Y$ given by the mapping diagram shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The domain of $g$ is $\\{1,2,3\\}$\n\nII. The codomain of $g$ is $\\{1,2,3\\}$\n\nIII. The range of $g$ is $\\{1,2,3\\}$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $g:X\\to Y$ means that the function $g$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $g$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $g$ is $Y.$\n\nThe range of $g$ is the subset of the codomain that contains all values $y\\in Y$ that are mapped to by at least one element $x\\in X.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect our diagram:\n\n- the domain of $g$ is $X=\\{1,2,3\\},$\n\n- the codomain of $g$ is $Y=\\{1,2,3,4\\},$\n\n- the range of $g$ is $\\{1,2,3\\},$ the subset of elements of $Y$ that have at least one incoming arrow.\n\nAs a result, statements I and III are true, while statement II is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240968 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172034 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1240e42a-92a5-51cc-a80e-3fe1ef0b16f7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Codomains and Ranges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cd5dc92-6122-5f66-902b-cf7afd5b3037" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461383} {:answer/id #uuid "a4df0471-5db6-5210-9df4-d5ba3514d0e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461384}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6cd5dc92-6122-5f66-902b-cf7afd5b3037" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461383} :answer-field/id #uuid "9cecc8bb-b961-5c98-b4f7-f3d1f9014f0a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462445} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2e1150f-e231-56c5-81bd-1920108321f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461385} {:answer/id #uuid "bb539ff7-3b84-5509-b986-80d61829af75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461386}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2e1150f-e231-56c5-81bd-1920108321f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461385} :answer-field/id #uuid "1efba3bd-1474-5f45-af9e-911cbd26dcba" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462446} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33626836-ef45-5786-b4be-68420909d8c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461387} {:answer/id #uuid "63330572-fab1-5f0e-845e-35f32466a424" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461388}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63330572-fab1-5f0e-845e-35f32466a424" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461388} :answer-field/id #uuid "65b7f335-a482-5fb5-99dc-778484b80d04" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462447}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "86293bdd-1445-5c75-b0fe-3a52f74f9647" :question/math-academy-id "q-104954" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8ff01afa1993f8e301edb69e3e7a13ee71ab01b1a3da416aa53ee94d021184e1.png)\n\nConsider the function $g:X\\to Y$ given by the mapping diagram shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The domain of $g$ is ${0,1,2}$\n- The codomain of $g$ is ${-1,0,1,2}$\n- The range of $g$ is ${0,1,2}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The notation $g:X\\to Y$ means that the function $g$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $g$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $g$ is $Y.$\n\nThe range of $g$ is the subset of the codomain that contains all values $y\\in Y$ that are mapped to by at least one element $x\\in X.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect our diagram:\n\n- the domain of $g$ is $X={-1,0,1,2},$\n\n- the codomain of $g$ is $Y={0,1,2},$\n\n- the range of $g$ is ${0,1,2},$ the subset of elements of $Y$ that have at least one incoming arrow.\n\nAs a result, statements I and II are false, while statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172627 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172031 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbac348b-629f-52a1-9bb3-0d3f8483ff44" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Arrow Function Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97015a4a-5c53-5378-8e05-a56fb36ed8e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171388} {:answer/id #uuid "976adf93-dc43-5e30-b5ea-cde860373a4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171389} {:answer/id #uuid "e1e3422f-e66e-5a42-bf7e-aabddf3aa8d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171390} {:answer/id #uuid "ef6cac8b-6d55-5f26-a7de-4324c08ad778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171391} {:answer/id #uuid "5e9e81b1-3ba2-53bf-a988-4476adbb310f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171392}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e9e81b1-3ba2-53bf-a988-4476adbb310f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171392} :answer-field/id #uuid "28bf7895-d26e-5e6f-91fd-70123c9ed2cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "56a15428-b444-550d-95b0-a7a76a54d0a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-96038" :question/problem "Which of the following functions has the codomain $[0,3)?$\n\nI. $p:\\{0,3\\}\\to R$\n\nII. $q:R\\to [0,3)$\n\nIII. $r:[0,3)\\to \\{0,3\\}$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $f:X\\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- The codomain of $p$ is $R,$ the set of real numbers.\n\n- The codomain of $q$ is $[0,3),$ the interval from $0$ to $3$ (including $0$ but not including $3$).\n\n- The codomain of $r$ is $\\{0,3\\},$ the set containing the two numbers $0$ and $3$ only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172630 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172033 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e61a3c8-45b0-532e-a54f-4597ad01a6f0" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Alternative Arrow Notations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answe